% Refusal is a first-class result. Every one of these fails cleanly and whole,
% with a position, so the caller can fall back to the evaluator.

### name: refuse.abort-is-an-effect-not-a-value
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
ABORT("nope")
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED 1:1
===
### name: refuse.list-in-scalar-position
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
(1, 2) + 1
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE 1:2
===
### name: refuse.list-returning-function
--- note
E_SQL_SHAPE rather than E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED: INDEXES is not missing from the map,
it is a function whose result is not a SQL value at all, in any dialect.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
INDEXES("a")
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE 1:1
===
### name: refuse.map-as-an-aggregate-source
--- note
FILTER only decides whether an element takes part, so binding both names to one
element absorbs it. MAP changes what the element is, so the two binders mean
different things. See docs/SQL-TRANSLATION.md §7.5 for the shape a solution
would take.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- source
ALL(MAP((1, 2), _ * 2), _ > 0)
--- error
E_SQL_UNSUPPORTED 1:5
===
### name: refuse.no-truthiness-in-a-condition
--- note
Silently accepting WHERE o.total is how a database turns a validation rule
into the truthiness test SEL spent its whole design avoiding.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- as
condition
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
T
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE
===
### name: refuse.no-truthiness-in-and
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
T AND TRUE
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE 1:1
===
### name: refuse.unknown-kind-may-be-a-condition
--- note
The other side of the same rule: an undeclared column may well be boolean, and
the database is the one that knows. It is wrapped rather than trusted.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- as
condition
--- bindings
{"F": {"kind": "column", "column": "flag"}}
--- source
F
--- expect
(`flag`) IS TRUE
===
### name: refuse.relation-binding-used-as-a-value
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ITEMS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "order_items", "alias": "oi", "fields": {"QTY": {"column": "qty", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ITEMS > 1
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE 1:1
===
### name: refuse.columns-binding-used-as-a-value
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"V": {"kind": "columns", "items": [{"column": "a", "type": "NUM"}, {"column": "b", "type": "NUM"}]}}
--- source
V > 1
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE 1:1
===
### name: refuse.unknown-field-of-a-relation
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"ITEMS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "order_items", "alias": "oi", "fields": {"QTY": {"column": "qty", "type": "NUM"}}}}
--- source
ITEMS["price"] > 1
--- error
E_SQL_BINDING 1:6
===
### name: refuse.alias-collision
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"A": {"kind": "relation", "from": "x", "alias": "t"}, "B": {"kind": "relation", "from": "y", "alias": "t"}}
--- source
1 + 1
--- error
E_SQL_BINDING
===
### name: refuse.indexing-a-plain-column
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"T": {"kind": "column", "column": "t", "type": "NUM"}}
--- source
T[1]
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE 1:2
### name: refuse.in-over-a-multi-field-relation
--- note
A relation with more than one field is a list of rows, and SEL compares a scalar
against a row structurally: FALSE for every row, always. Projecting one column
would translate something the evaluator never answers.

Found by the row oracle: the server selected order 1 and SEL selected nothing.
The doc's own example (§7.6) was this shape. Bind the projected column as its
own one-field relation, which sql/oracle/rows.json now does.
--- dialect
mariadb
--- bindings
{"SKU": {"kind": "column", "table": "o", "column": "sku", "type": "TEXT"}, "ITEMS": {"kind": "relation", "from": "order_items", "alias": "oi", "correlate": {"raw": "`oi`.`order_id` = `o`.`id`"}, "scalar": "SKU", "fields": {"QTY": {"table": "oi", "column": "qty", "type": "NUM"}, "SKU": {"table": "oi", "column": "sku", "type": "TEXT"}}}}
--- source
SKU IN ITEMS
--- error
E_SQL_SHAPE
===
